From: strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight)
Subject: Re: [cfp94.kadie] Applying library principles to computers
Message-ID: <strnlghtCnE2y8.JzG@netcom.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Mar 1994 19:18:07 GMT


I've read through Carl Kadie's talk with great interest. It's a nice format
for presentation, assuming one thinks the general principles of the
librarians should apply to electronic networks accessible by individuals
without physically presenting themselves.

However, Carl seems to me to use some of the principles in an ambiguous way
to support his views, without presenting evidence that the language of the
librarians applies to the case he's applying it to.

I'll give one brief example. He cites the librarians' policy against
"prejudicial labels" in discussing Congressman Markey's U-Chip bill which
calls for a content signal to be transmitted with certain materials so
parents may, if they choose, censor such materials from their children.

But he has made no showing that the labels proposed by Markey are
"prejudicial". If violence is defined clearly, and the label is applied
even-handedly to any material containing violence, then it is a content
label, not a prejudicial label.

The librarians continue to label sexual non-fiction according to the Dewey
Decimal system or the Library of Congress cataloging system. Thus labelling
content cannot be considered prejudicial. One commonly finds bookstores (and
maybe some libraries) having separate sections for categories of fiction,
viz Mystery, Science-Fiction, Romance, etc. This isn't thought to be
prejudicial.

I don't want to go into a long thing about Carl's talk, particularly since I
though it was, on balance, quite good, but there are a few other areas where
he seems to me to be using the librarians' guidelines where they don't
clearly apply. Finding those is left to the interested and careful reader.

David



