[...]
>But he has made no showing that the labels proposed by Markey are
>"prejudicial". If violence is defined clearly, and the label is applied
>even-handedly to any material containing violence, then it is a content
>label, not a prejudicial label.
>
>The librarians continue to label sexual non-fiction according to the Dewey
>Decimal system or the Library of Congress cataloging system.
[...]

I'm pretty sure that the American Library Association's Office of
Intellectual Freedom the V-chip proposal involves prejudicial
labeling. (I guess we could ask, if you really wanted to know for sure.)

As for library subject classification, it is not without problems.
There is aan on going discussion on the ALA-OIF list about whether
so-called creation science books should be classified as Science or
Bible Studies. There is also a Library of Congress category for
"History-errors, inventions, etc."

My rule of thumb for "What is prejudicial?" is "Would the creators
object to the label?". Your book _Annual review of energy_ Vol. 10 is
labeled "1.  Power resources 2.  Energy conservation." here at U. of
Illinois. My master's thesis is labeled "1. Machine learning.  2.
Robotics.". I doubt that either of us find these labels objectionable.

I would, however, object to a government-compelled "illegal in China"
label, even if it was applied even-handedly to any material that would
in fact be illegal in China.

- Carl

