I have never used a two week expulsion for anything here.  I have
occasionally suspended user accounts for 1 week.  Usually this was after
they had received a warning and had ignored it.  Most commonly the
offense was leaving a significant number of looping processes running,
which interfered with response time for other users.  The student is
advised that, if he is unhappy with the suspension, he should discuss
it with his instructor.  When requested by an instructor, I do normally
reinstate the account.

Incidently, I don't consider such actions to be "punishment."  Rather
it is a way of educating the student.  Problems more commonly result
from naivite than from malice.

One effect of the reference to instructors is that the instructors get
feedback on what they are not teaching students, and usually they use
this to adjust what they cover in their courses.

>                                                                    If
>you think the student or professor is creditable threat to the system
>pending the hearing, you should get approval of a Dean for an interim
>emergency suspension.

Generally speaking, the Dean's familiarity with the computer is as
a budget item.  It is often better to avoid referring minor problems
to the Dean.

>This is better than I feared, but still not as good as having
>disciplinary policy approved by those who typically have authority to
>approve disciplinary policy: the Senate, the Chancellor, or the
>Trustees (Board of Governors).

If, as I suggest, problems are more commonly the result of naivete than
of malice, if makes little sense to refer the problem to the Dean or to
a disciplinary committee.  That should be an option if the student
insists.  But more often a simple commonsense informal solution is
better, and that way the student does not find an entry in his record,
and the Dean does not hear of the student over an embarrassing incident
which is better forgotten by all.


