In article <2na0mg$4ds@news.iastate.edu>,
John Hascall <john@iastate.edu> wrote:
>David Sternlight <david@sternlight.com> wrote:
>
>}The librarians continue to label sexual non-fiction according to the Dewey
>}Decimal system or the Library of Congress cataloging system. Thus labelling
>}content cannot be considered prejudicial.
>
>   Cataloging is not the same thing.  Cataloging is essentially ordering.
>   Everything has a catalog number.  Certain items are not `called out'.
>   A "QA76.9" and an "HB46.9" are in a sense "equal".
>
   In fact, both posters seem to be talking about classification, or
   shelf-numbering, rather than cataloguing. The shelf numbers,
   Dewey or LC, do a number of things, but mainly they serve to
   make sure that books on similar topics can be found near one another
   on the shelves. 

   By cataloguing, I really mean subject analysis... which is done
   according to Sears Subject Headings in small places, or the
   Library of Congress Subject Headings in large ones. It's here
   that some biases may come into play, since a book on religion, for
   example, the Mass book of the RC church, might look like this in the West:

   Catholic church -- Liturgy and ritual -- Missal.

   In the old Soviet system, the same book would probably have
   been given a heading in  the "Superstition" hierarchy.

   Dewey and LC class systems attempt to break down knowledge into
   various categories (10 major ones for Dewey, hence the "decimal"
   orientation). But in terms of retrieval, most people use the
   class scheme when browsing the shelves, not when looking things
   up in the computer. The subject headings, however, act as search
   terms or "access points" and are more likely to be of immediate
   use to people who aren't familiar with classification, IMHO.
   
   that's a rough & ready explanation, of course, and not entirely
    by the book....
