In article <2naak3$rfr@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu>, dougmc@utpapa.ph.utexas.edu (Doug McLaren) writes:
> Are chain letters even a problem?
> I've gotten "With sex all things are possible" at least 15 times, and while
> it was cute the first time, I just now 'd' it.   And I don't get upset about
> it ...

There are two problems actually.  One is the space problem that has already
been described.  Enforcing quotas can help here, but even then more important
mail might get bounced.  The second is the complaints about junk mail to the
system admin.  When one of these things is going around on a network of
thousands of users, an admin may get literally dozens to hundreds of complaints
daily.  Responding to those complaints takes valuable time that could be more
usefully spent doing other things.

I won't mention any names here (to protect the innocent), but I recently
observed what I thought was an interesting solution to this problem at an
academic site.  The "ST JUDE" letter was making the rounds.  Students (mostly)
were passing it around like a hotcake, and faculty and researchers were
complaining loudly about the junk mail filling up their mailboxes.  A special
login notice was setup containing the usual stuff from the academic usage
guidelines about possible penalties for abuse of computing resources, and
requesting anyone receiving a chain letter to send a copy of it to a
particular mailbox address.  Faculty members and researchers were pleased
because they had a place to send their complaints specifically about the chain
letters.  Students immediately knew what was going to happen and began to
stop sending chain letter mail.  I guess it took about a week to die out
completely.  It was pretty painless except for a few complaints about the
login notice.  The problem was stopped quickly, no one lost their accounts,
and everyone lived happily ever after...  well, at least until next time...
