I've suggested that as a way to deal with potentially offensive material, 
as well as anything which is too bulky to do a mass mailing or to post
in the login bulletins. Post a description: anyone who wants it can read
it and anyone who doesn't can skip it without offense. Everyone gets
a forum for free speech; everyone gets to choose whether they want to 
listen, and the poster is protected from the P.C. Police.

  It is true that there is less due process in harassment cases than in 
anything else (except maybe tax fraud.) The Political Correctness
Committees have their own justice system and their own rules. We're living
in hysterical times. The best solution I have is to allow everyone to speak
but allow others to not listen if they choose. 

  This is how Universities work, at their best. You allow anyone who
wants to speak  to use a lecture hall or to set up a literature table. You
do not allow others to disrupt lectures or trash the tables. You do not
let people interrupt classes with bullhorns or pull the plug on a speaker's
microphone. Everyone gets free speech but some order is maintained. I think
Usenet should work the same way: give everyone a forum, but let people
choose whether to read it. We should give Sergar Ardic a group, and
everyone should agree to carry it, and he should be limited to the charter
topic in other groups. I think this is analagous to allowing him to set
up a literature table in the student center, but not allowing him to put
his literature all over everyone else's table.

