While I sympathise with those who wish to post in civil disobedience of the
ban, this is a two edged sword.

The Holmolka news ban has become the thin end of the wedge for people who
want to ban newsgroups in Canada.  You see, they start by banning the
newsgroup devoted to the trial, and those who would fight bans have a
very hard time fighting this one, because the law is cut and dried.

Ie. you can disagree with the law all you want, but it doesn't change
the fact that the banned material is illegal.

Problem is, with one group banned for illegal material, people now have
a weapon to ban others, which is how various groups got banned at UW.

So the acts of civil disobedience do make the point that you can't enforce
the ban, and that's a good point to make, but they also provide a
tool for the banners to shut off more and more newsgroups.

It's a no win situation.  It's proof that if you let them ban even one,
they'll take more.  But Canadians didn't get a choice on this first one,
it's a court order.   Well, the court order is being appealed of course,
by powerful media forces, but for now there is no choice.
