>     There is/was a court ordered media blackout in Canada on the
>Teale-Homolka case in order not to prejudice a related case that
>had yet to come to trial.  Obviously the blackout did not apply
>to US media.  Once both trials are over, the ban will not be needed.
>I think the right to a fair trial is the greater concern in this
>case.  The media can capitalize on these people's misery later, provided
>the public still cares.

That's not the point.  The point is that Karla's trial was a secret trial,
which is a direct violation of the constitutional right to a PUBLIC trial. 
The media ban is sweeping enough that publication of her plea is
illegal--but seriously:  it's easy to figure out what the plea was, given
that a) she was busted on manslaughter and her hubby is accused of murder
one; b) the trial lasted only two hours; and c) there was a joint
sentencing submission given by the Crown and defence.  Because of the
media gag, it's hard to tell from everything that's going around just
what's fact and what's fiction.  Lifting the ban NOW will clear the air
once and for all.  Delaying it will only allow rumours to continue long
after the trial's over.
	As far as McMaster University is concerned, I have to say this: 
universities should be places for free and public debate of a wide range
of issues.  The idea that microfilm--which often contain valuable
historical information--should be censored, not just temporarily, but
apparently for all time, is absolutely stupid.  Sure, all the stuff is on
INTERNET if you, or I, or anyone else wants it.  But many people do not
have, or do not want to have, access to computers.  They, like many
netters, just want to see that justice has in fact been served.  Without
an opportunity to examine the facts NOW, it's almost impossible to say
whether Homolka's plea bargain was fair or even appropriate.  Seems to me
that the state is hiding something--especially now that Marion Boyd has
ordered a direct indictment (which may be a violation of the right to
fundamental justice).
	As for the fair trial issue:  it's bullshit that a person has to
be completely ignorant of a case to be a reliable juror.  Just look what
happened in the states not too long ago in the Rodney King cases.  The TV
networks showed King being beaten up, over and over and over again, till
everyone knew who did it.  Did that prejudice the officer's right to a
fair trial?  NO!  In both the state assault trial and the federal civil
rights trial, 12 honest and reasonable people were found who weighed the
evidence and came to an unbiased decision.
	In the same vein, even if everyone knows the facts in this
case--and just about everyone does by now, despite what Marion Boyd
thinks--the fact remains that jurors take an oath to weigh the evidence
and come to a fair decision, based solely on facts presented at the trial. 
They set aside whatever hunches they have at the beginning of the trial
and start with a fresh slate the moment the accused makes his or her plea. 
	It doesn't matter if Homolka told the truth to the police or if
she acted alone.  The fact remains, this would have disappeared off the
front pages a long time ago if Kovacs didn't issue the gag order.  The
families have been through hell, and the ban only forces them to continue
to live through hell.  Getting it on the record NOW is the first step in
the healing process.
	Obviously, these are horrendous crimes we're talking about, and I
feel a sense of anger whenever random acts of violence like this
happen--particularly to young women.  Unfortunately, it's that very fact
that makes it clear that you just can't gag the truth.  The people have to
know the nature of the crimes now, before the trial begins.  Truly, 
McMaster University deserves to be on the WALL OF SHAME.
----------------------
Robert Pavlacic
McMaster University
Hamilton, Ontario
