I am in complete agreement about the nastiness of bureaucratic runarounds,
but as Carl points out, if you break more rules after being accused of
breaking a rule, you have played into their hands. 

  Doesn't your school have an ombudsperson? You should be able to go to the
Dean of Students with a complaint like that. This sort of thing is always
in the fine print in your student handbook (look in the official
Catalogue.) Also,try the Academic Advising office, or your advisor, or your
department chair. Bureaucrats aren't always going to TELL you what steps to
take, but if there is a big bureaucracy, you ought to be able to find at
least one professor or professional staff person who likes to challenge the
system.

  My guess is that sometimes students give up too soon, and think that
breaking back into the computer system will be easier than breaking through
the bureaucracy. But, you have to learn how to navigate the bureaucracy
sooner or later. Think of it as a valuable lesson. Bureaucracies have their
own psychology and there always IS a way through the maze. 

