>That's not the point.  The point is that Karla's trial was a secret trial,

No it wasn't.  The media was present, we know what the charges were, the
fact that she was convicted, and the sentence.  And in not too much longer
we'll know the rest.

>... Because of the
>media gag, it's hard to tell from everything that's going around just
>what's fact and what's fiction.

Precisely.  We have a system of fair trial here.  Where people are tried
based upon what is presented in a court of law.  Not convicted simply because
the vast illinformed public thinks they should be as happens in the USA.

>	As for the fair trial issue:  it's bullshit that a person has to
>be completely ignorant of a case to be a reliable juror.  Just look what
>happened in the states not too long ago in the Rodney King cases.  The TV
>networks showed King being beaten up, over and over and over again, till
>everyone knew who did it.  Did that prejudice the officer's right to a
>fair trial?  NO!  In both the state assault trial and the federal civil
>rights trial, 12 honest and reasonable people were found who weighed the
>evidence and came to an unbiased decision.

This is a joke right?  Or did it escape your notice that the two sets
of 12 honest and reasonable people came up with opposite decisions?
The first trial was vastly unfair, with the whole country politically
pressurized and howling for their heads.  The second trial was a
travesty - how can a juror come up with an unbiased decision while
knowing that if they choose innocent, the whole country might go up in
flames and dozens or hundreds of people die all over again?

The Rodney King incident shows quite adequately how an "absolutely open
and free press" can trample over the rights of an accused person.  All
you need to do is make a big enough stink, and the government will keep
retrying them until the verdict is satisfactory to the public.

In Canada, if publication had been allowed to the extent it was in the
US, the first trial would have probably mistrialed.  The second would
have never taken place.

In Canada, however, they would have only allowed the tapes to be shown once
or twice, and there wouldn't have been anywhere near as much political
pressure on the first jury.

In contrast, in the USA, that a few billion dollars in damage and 50+ deaths
are the price they *want* to pay for a piddly little assault conviction just
so they can get their instant hit of TV entertainment.

>	In the same vein, even if everyone knows the facts in this
>case--and just about everyone does by now, despite what Marion Boyd
>thinks--the fact remains that jurors take an oath to weigh the evidence
>and come to a fair decision, based solely on facts presented at the trial. 
>They set aside whatever hunches they have at the beginning of the trial
>and start with a fresh slate the moment the accused makes his or her plea. 

If you believe that, I have a bridge to sell you.

>	It doesn't matter if Homolka told the truth to the police or if
>she acted alone.  The fact remains, this would have disappeared off the
>front pages a long time ago if Kovacs didn't issue the gag order.  The
>families have been through hell, and the ban only forces them to continue
>to live through hell.  Getting it on the record NOW is the first step in
>the healing process.

Speak for yourself.  The families have come out in support of the ban....
