CICA's Windows archive is distributed via CD-ROM.  It is, by far, the largest
compendium of Windows software I know of.  I would not be surprised if the
number of people who get most of their software from CICA and from nowhere
else numbers in the thousands, if not the tens of thousands.  Thus, the
removal of Fanny Hill *greatly* reduced its distribution.  Maybe in the future,
when the network is fully established in the world, and CD-ROM distribution
is less needed, these issues will no longer be important.  But, until then...


As far as the New Zealand Censorship Society and Weekend Cabal, well they
did FORCE, in hindsight, CICA to remove Fanny Hill.  If they wouldn't have
spoken up (and no one else), Fanny would have been quietly archived and found
its way onto the CD-ROM.  It is interesting that they were the *only
ones* to complain, and my private e-mail with several people in New Zealand
has convinced me that their culture, as a whole with notable exceptions,
still lives in the 19th century when it comes to censorship issues and how
the society/culture handles "sensitive" material.  Though it is their right
to live as they please, they show a great lack of sensitivity to the different
social climate in other parts of the world.  They firmly believe they have a
moral duty to lead the rest of the world into "enlightenment", which means
strict control of content with full government blessing (why is it that,
until recently and maybe they still do, the NZ government would not allow
private radio/tv broadcasting?  This is something you'd find in Soviet-style
socialism and other dictatorships.)

This is the biggest immorality I can imagine, particulary the original post
from the person in NZ pointing out to Regoli at CICA with a smug
authoritarian air that Fanny Hill was unsuitable/whatever, as if he knew what
was best for CICA.  There wasn't even a policy statement at CICA that would
forbid such material!, so on what legitimate basis could he say that it was
unsuitable?  If he was so bothered by it, the appropriate and moral response
by him would have been no response -- just quit downloading those "forbidden"
files from CICA or find a "clean" site that fits his tastes better.  Better
yet, start a new one.

As I've said before, I don't believe that Regoli at CICA had any personal
difficulty with Fanny Hill (at least he's never said anything) -- but he was
obviously scared "shitless" that any adverse publicity could close his baby
down.  I find the real culprit to be the University who owns the facilities 
in not supporting a strong anti-censorship, library-like statement *once* they
found out about the whole incident (I think they preferred that it all blow
over and maintain the status quo -- being reactive rather than pro-active).

I call upon them, again, to institute a clear anti-censorship policy for the
CICA Windows archive, similar in scope and structure to the ALA one (American
Library Association, I believe).  The current one, which is unbelievably
restrictive for a *public* site run by *public* money (I'll reproduce it in
a separate post), is obviously a far-cry from that, and is a blot on the
reputation of the University.  And I encourage them to post to the net and
explain why they can justify the present CICA Windows archive policy.  If
they can't, then I suggest they give Regoli the authority *and* encouragement
to write one that fits the progressive nature of their University and the ALA
statement.  I'm sure if he is given the blessing that he would rise to the
occasion and write a *real* and progressive policy that will do justice to
his archive, as well as to keeping the future "world network" open and free.
Do we want to see the "old-way of doing things" (censorship) being used to
define the future of the World Net?  I see bigger issues at stake here than
the hubbub over a file called "Fanny Hill".

Jon Noring

