Michael without IU approval, is borderline illegal for a State University.

And I encourage IU to take the positive step of affirming a "no censorship"
ALA-like policy (CICA *is* an electronic library!), so people within and
without the U.S. (whether in NZ, or Saudi Arabia, or wherever) cannot dictate
the speech content of the CICA electronic library.  At this time, Michael
Regoli is so scared about losing CICA that he is willing to kow-tow to any
group in the world who believe it is their moral right to dictate the content
of any library in the world.  I'd say it is better for CICA to be closed down
by IU than to allow special interest groups to dictate what CICA, a publicly
supported library, cannot carry purely based on speech content.  The
ramifications of this go well beyond Fanny Hill/CICA, and will touch upon the
future "World Net".

Jon Noring


